+% Generated by reproduction/general_birkhoff_experiments/build_assets.py.
+\subsection{General Birkhoff comparison: objects, solves and costs}
+\label{gsn:protocol}
+The additional calculations are in
+\texttt{reproduction/general\_birkhoff\_experiments}.
+From the package root they are run by
+\begin{verbatim}
+cd reproduction
+cd general_birkhoff_experiments
+python run_all.py
+\end{verbatim}
+The outputs are \path{magnetic_method_comparison.json},
+\path{synthetic_method_comparison.json} and
+\path{run_validation.json} in that directory. Each retains source
+hashes, the parameter version, all step sizes, full error series,
+solver diagnostics and measured times. Existing material-A FE, fitting,
+four-group restoration/tracking and damping-scan records are retained
+from their completed calculations; they are not described as newly
+rerun by this added comparison. The physical baseline file and all
+those existing numerical JSON records are checked to remain unchanged.
+
+\subsubsection{The magnetic specializations}
+The physical comparison loads the same $\mathsf N$, $d$, endpoint
+inertia and SI scales as \eqref{num:scales}. Its single field within
+each row group is
+\begin{equation}
+ \begin{split}
+ X'&=\mathsf NY,\\
+ Y'&=-\mathsf NX-\kappa d[\sin(d^{\mathsf T}X)-d^{\mathsf T}X]
+       -\gamma Y.
+ \end{split}
+ \label{gsn:magnetic-field}
+\end{equation}
+The three pairs are $(\kappa,\gamma)=(1.2,0.05)$,
+$(1.02,0.01)$ and $(1,0)$, respectively. They describe the already
+declared synthetic plant, fixed-error closed-field diagnostic and
+conservative target. Known coefficients in a method test are not
+additional information supplied to the learning controller. Every
+case starts at $(0.09,0.08,0.015,-0.01)$, ends at $\tau=4$, and uses
+$\Delta=0.04,0.02,0.01,0.005$. These coordinates and time are
+dimensionless; physical time is $t=\tau/\omega_0$.
+
+The chart implementation advances $u=(X,P)$ with
+$P=e^{\gamma\tau}Y$. At the temporal midpoint its field is
+\begin{equation}
+ \begin{gathered}
+ F_\chi(u,\tau_m)=
+ \binom{e^{-\gamma\tau_m}\mathsf NP}
+ {-e^{\gamma\tau_m}\nabla_X V_\kappa(X)},\\
+ V_\kappa=\tfrac12X^{\mathsf T}\mathsf NX+\kappa g(d^{\mathsf T}X).
+ \end{gathered}
+ \label{gsn:magnetic-chart-field}
+\end{equation}
+Here $F_\chi$ denotes the transformed vector field, and
+$g(s)=1-\cos s-s^2/2$ is the previously defined potential feature.
+The code solves this canonical midpoint equation and transforms back
+at $\tau+\Delta$; a separate implementation solves the weighted
+physical-coordinate equations directly. It compares their entire
+trajectories. At $\gamma=0$ both reduce to ordinary midpoint.
+Central4 uses the complete nonlinear derivatives in
+\eqref{ss:central4-magnetic}--\eqref{ss:central4-magnetic-jacobian}
+only for $(\kappa,\gamma)=(1,0)$.
+
+Independent original-coordinate DOP853 references use the two settings
+specified in Supplementary Section~\ref{sx:framework-closed}.
+The endpoint and maximum-grid Euclidean errors have separate observed
+orders; refinement of the reference is recorded separately from those
+errors. The tested structure is $\mathcal K(\tau)=e^{\gamma\tau}\mathcal K_0$,
+$\mathcal K_0=-J$. The JSON also records the equivalent exponential conformal
+defect using $J$, whose normalization differs by the absolute-time factor.
+
+\subsubsection{Variable mass and nonproportional drag}
+The independent synthetic model uses the point map
+\begin{equation}
+ \begin{split}
+ Q_1&=q_1+0.2q_1^3,\qquad Q_2=(1+0.15q_1)q_2,\\
+ D_Q&=D_qQ,\qquad P=D_Q^{-\mathsf T}p,\qquad
+ \xi(q,p)=(Q,P).
+ \end{split}
+ \label{gsn:point-chart}
+\end{equation}
+All the following numbers are dimensionless test choices:
+\begin{equation}
+ \begin{gathered}
+ M_0=\begin{pmatrix}1.12&0.12\\0.12&1.32\end{pmatrix},\quad
+ K_s=\begin{pmatrix}1.05&0.05\\0.05&2.05\end{pmatrix},\\
+ C_0=\begin{pmatrix}0.18&0.025\\0.025&0.37\end{pmatrix},\quad
+ C_d=\begin{pmatrix}0.09&0.009\\0.009&0.14\end{pmatrix},\\
+ K_p=\diag(0.05,0.10).
+ \end{gathered}
+ \label{gsn:synthetic-matrices}
+\end{equation}
+For a selected pair $(C_s,S_s)$, the original mechanical coefficients are
+$M(q)=D_Q^{\mathsf T}M_0D_Q$,
+$C(q)=D_Q^{\mathsf T}C_sD_Q$, and
+$V(q)=Q^{\mathsf T}S_sQ/2$. Direct symbolic differentiation of
+$H=p^{\mathsf T}M(q)^{-1}p/2+V(q)$ retains every term in $H_q$;
+the ordinary-midpoint field is evaluated from these mechanical
+equations. Independently, the canonical point chart gives
+\begin{equation}
+ \begin{gathered}
+ y'=A_sy,\qquad y=\xi(z),\\
+ A_s=\begin{pmatrix}0&M_0^{-1}\\-S_s&-C_sM_0^{-1}\end{pmatrix}.
+ \end{gathered}
+ \label{gsn:latent-field}
+\end{equation}
+The two cases use $(C_s,S_s)=(C_0,K_s)$ and
+$(C_d,K_s+K_p)$. Their common initial state is
+$(0.25,-0.18,0.03,0.04)$, time interval is $[0,2]$, and steps
+are $0.2,0.1,0.05,0.025$. Both have configuration-dependent mass
+and nonproportional drag. Sampled mass eigenvalues and chart
+denominators are reported; these are not global domain certificates.
+
+The prescribed structure, fixed before discretization, is
+\begin{equation}
+ \begin{split}
+ \mathcal K_y(t)&=e^{-A_s^{\mathsf T}t}\mathcal K_0e^{-A_st},\\
+ \mathcal K_z(z,t)&=D\xi(z)^{\mathsf T}\mathcal K_y(t)D\xi(z).
+ \end{split}
+ \label{gsn:specified-K}
+\end{equation}
+The code uses the real principal branch
+$T(t)=(\mathcal K_0^{-1}\mathcal K_y(t))^{1/2}$ and checks its congruence.
+Differentiating $T^2=\mathcal K_0^{-1}\mathcal K_y$ gives the Sylvester equation
+$T\dot T+\dot TT=\mathcal K_0^{-1}\dot{\mathcal K}_y$, where
+$\dot{\mathcal K}_y=-A_s^{\mathsf T}\mathcal K_y-\mathcal K_yA_s$.
+Thus $\chi_t=T(t)\xi$ has transformed Hamiltonian matrix
+$L(t)=\dot TT^{-1}+TA_sT^{-1}$. The numerical state update is
+\begin{equation}
+ \begin{split}
+ C_m&=(I-\Delta L_m/2)^{-1}(I+\Delta L_m/2),\\
+ y_+&=T(t+\Delta)^{-1}C_mT(t)y_-,\\
+ z_+&=\xi^{-1}(y_+),\qquad L_m=L(t+\Delta/2).
+ \end{split}
+ \label{gsn:synthetic-update}
+\end{equation}
+This update uses a midpoint Cayley matrix, not $e^{A_s\Delta}$.
+Matrix exponentials are required to construct the known $\mathcal K_y$ and
+to obtain the independent exact-conjugacy reference
+$\xi^{-1}(e^{A_st}\xi(z_0))$. The latter is additionally compared
+with original-coordinate DOP853. Such an explicit structure is a
+special advantage of this constructed example, not a general cheap
+solution of the one-form transport equation.
+
+The ideal synthetic control is
+\begin{equation}
+ U_q=D_Q^{\mathsf T}
+       [(C_0-C_d)M_0^{-1}P-K_pQ].
+ \label{gsn:synthetic-control}
+\end{equation}
+Substitution changes the uncontrolled pair to $(C_d,K_s+K_p)$.
+Both control groups are assessed against the same exact controlled
+target from the common initial state at step $0.025$.
+The force is ideal and fully actuated in these two synthetic coordinates;
+it has no learned coefficient, magnetic decoder or saturation constraint.
+The test checks the actual closed-loop $\mathcal K_u$ and separately demonstrates
+its difference from the original $\mathcal K$. It does not replace the four
+magnetic control groups in Tables~\ref{num:a-table} and~\ref{num:b-table}.
+
+\subsubsection{Residuals, representative tangents and measured cost}
+All nonlinear midpoint increments use residual infinity tolerance
+$2\times10^{-14}$ and at most $12$ Newton corrections, with failure
+terminating the calculation. The synthetic chart midpoint is a linear
+solve, so its nonlinear-increment Newton count/residual is reported as
+not applicable. The code instead measures its linear chart increment
+residual, the Cayley matrix equation residual, and the nonlinear
+$\xi^{-1}$ coordinate residual. The scalar inverse point map uses a
+function-residual tolerance $5\times10^{-15}$ and at most $30$
+corrections. Residuals in different coordinates are named separately;
+their conversion requires the chart-conditioning bounds in the theory.
+
+The maximum ideal-tangent structure defect is measured at every saved
+step. Independent centered differences of the implemented step are
+evaluated only at representative states: the magnetic checks use
+$\tau=0.36$, $\Delta=0.02$,
+$z=z_0+0.001(0,1,2,3)^{\mathsf T}$ and differencing scales
+$10^{-4},10^{-5},10^{-6}$ for every method and field.
+The synthetic chart checks use $t=0$, $\Delta=0.1$,
+$z=z_0+0.01(0,1,2,3)^{\mathsf T}$ and scales
+$2\times10^{-4},2\times10^{-5},2\times10^{-6}$.
+The epsilon sweeps expose finite-difference and rounding effects;
+they are not full-domain derivative enclosures. The synthetic tests
+also evaluate the endpoint compatibility block identity directly.
+
+Timings use one BLAS thread, one unmeasured dispatch warmup and three
+measured repetitions in the same process. The reported value is the
+median; all individual times are stored. Cold time contains required
+method preparation and trajectory advancement. For a synthetic grid of
+$N_s$ steps, this includes $2N_s+1$ matrix exponentials and principal
+square roots, $N_s$ Sylvester solves, and the dense linear solves forming
+the complete update cache. Warm time explicitly reuses that cache.
+The one-off symbolic mechanical-field/Jacobian construction is reported
+separately. Independent references and geometric/tangent diagnostics
+are excluded consistently from method timing. These costs describe
+this implementation and do not establish a universal speed advantage.
+
